From 775ec4e1b80868fdb8e2afb1776c3e8434e30027 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: royka1 <397414+royka1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 13:25:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 70/74] media: qcom: camss: add C-PHY support

Every lane table in the CSIPHY driver is a D-PHY one, the endpoint parser
rejects anything that is not D-PHY outright, and CSI2_RX_CFG0's PHY type
field is defined but never assigned, so camss can only receive D-PHY. The
wide camera on the Xiaomi Mi 10T is a C-PHY sensor, which leaves its PHY
unable to train: it reports none of the lock bits a working link sets, and
the CSID receiver sees no traffic at all, not even error bits.

Teach the driver the other standard. C-PHY carries its clock inside each
trio, so there is no clock lane to describe, and a trio takes one odd bit
in the lane enable where a D-PHY lane takes an even one. A few of the PHY
settings depend on how fast the link runs, so derive the rate on the wire
from the link frequency -- C-PHY moves 16 bits per 7 symbols per trio,
which the tables express as 2.28 bits per symbol -- and apply the first
bucket that covers it.

The register sequences come from the vendor driver for this PHY revision,
except for the rate dependent ones, which were taken from what the vendor
kernel actually writes on this board: the published source for that table
disagrees with the shipped kernel, so the device was believed instead.
Only the slowest bucket is described, which is the one a 4.1 Gbit/s link
selects.

Also handle the high settle count byte. Nothing needed it until now,
because no D-PHY table uses it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
